great stuff so look these uh results  today much better than the market  expected uh  how much of that is down to what you  would describe as self-help and how much  has it been flattered by stronger oil  and gas prices in the quarter  well ian thanks for having me on they  are a good set of results um clearly  financially a strong set of earnings but  importantly i think a strong set of uh  of cash numbers uh we talk in 11 billion  dollars of cash in the quarter that's  about  six from operations and about five from  divestment proceeds and what that's  enabled us to do is get our net debt  down about a year  ahead of schedule um down below our  target of 35 we're actually at 33.3  billion this morning that's down 18  billion dollars  from its peak in the pandemic and that's  allowed us then to start buybacks  and of course at the same time we've  made strategic progress so  the reasons why are it's been a we did  have an exceptional  quarter in gas trading the environment's  clearly been  helpful to us and the underlying  business is performing  uh very well castrol for example had a  very good quarter so  it is down to the hard work that the  team has done over the last 12  months focused on the balance sheet  focused on cost focused on capital  focused on bringing those projects  online as you say net debt down by 5.6  billion to 33.3 billion  the market's been very concerned about  debt levels do you think this is going  to be enough to put to bed those worries  well i think it's a i think today is a  real milestone in that regard we have  as i said down 5.6 billion in the  quarter down 18 billion dollars from the  peak of 51 billion dollars last year  so it's been a step-by-step journey  focused on the basics of the business  and i do think it's a very strong signal  to the market  uh that net debt is very much going in  the right direction  what we've said ian is that we will  start buybacks in the second quarter  and that going forward for this year  we'll allocate 60  of any surplus cash to buybacks and we  will continue to take care of the  balance sheet allocating 40  of surplus cash to the balance sheet so  in a moderate environment we should  expect that  uh trajectory to continue now in june  last year unanimous plans to  cut 10 000 jobs globally how far along  the road are you in that process  well it's been a very painful process  obviously not least for those involved  and for all the people around them five  and a half thousand people  have left the company so far about  another two to three thousand will leave  this year and the remainder  will leave at the beginning of next year  uh great people who've dedicated their  their lives 30 to 40 years in many cases  to the company and  we obviously wish them well into the  future i think the good  news here ian is that the restructuring  is essentially behind us  we have done what we needed to do the  new organization is up and running  we're now focused on the business at  hand running the operations  running the marketing business and doing  the things that we need to do  to deliver shareholders what they want  which is a  competitive cash return which today  marks that milestone on the buybacks  while at the same time not a choice  while at the same time transitioning the  bp for the future now during the quarter  you and your partner spent nearly a  billion pounds on buying the rights to  develop two offshore wind sites  in the irish sea a lot of people thought  you overpaid for those assets  well not in the least and uh if i got a  a  pound or a penny for the people who have  since  wanted to come in and buy into what we  just won  um i think i'd be in a very good place  so  others seem to think that it was a very  good deal and are willing to put their  money  where their mouth is so to speak we're  not farming down those interests we're  very very pleased  it's a very different uh business to  what  was available on the the other side of  the uh  the british isles and in the same lease  round great location  great wind resource synergistic uh  blocks next to each other um hopefully  shorter licensing times because of the  the migratory bird patterns and so on  and so forth  so it's like the same house on a good  street and a not so good street  and you know it's going to cost you a  little bit more on the good street and  this is definitely a good street so i'm  very very pleased  that we were successful in that license  round uh and look forward to developing  um those leases in the months and years  ahead and hopefully creating a lot of  good jobs in the process  your opposite number at total has said  that he's concerned there's a bubble in  some renewable assets is there a danger  that people  are overpaying for some of these assets  look i i'm not going to comment on on  what others do all i can do is look at  what we do ourselves and  you know the more i learn about this  part of the industry and the more  convinced i am  that we have a lot to add to deliver the  sorts of return  promises that we've made our investors  eight to ten percent returns  these are 60-year leases lifetime of 60  years  this is stable cash flow for generations  in many many regards  and we can bring all of the experience  that we've learned in the offshore  hydrocarbon business and apply it to the  offshore wind business  we can use our supply chain organization  that we built up here in the uk over  decades  and importantly as we look at hydrogen  on the east coast we can  take that wind power and use it to to  maybe power the hydrogen facility  we have bp pulse here in the uk i think  the largest charging  ev charging business here in the uk we  can use the  uh the the electrons generated by the  offshore wind to power  our own charging business so when you  look at it in the round  it really is what we call an integrated  energy company strategy  in action yes you mentioned uh bp pulse  when will capex peak for this particular  part of the business  well i think we will continue to grow  this business as demand  grows over the coming years we will be  disciplined of course as we do that  but i'm very excited about it and we're  doing a lot of work on  on-the-go charging we're going to put  500 ultra-fast chargers this is where  you can get a 100 miles in 10 minutes  uh 150 kilowatts chargers in  in germany we're building out our  position here in the uk we got 10 000  charge points here in the uk  and in china ian we're partnered with  didi which is the world's largest  mobility provider 550 million users  30 million rides a day we've close to 2  000 charge points with dd  in in china they're the number one owner  of electric vehicles in the world so  we've just signed deals this past  quarter with the volkswagen group  to accelerate that rule out in europe  we've got deals with daimler  i'm very excited about this part of the  business as the world transitions  uh to evs as is happening now it's more  than a year since united's water  generally regarded as the most ambitious  plans by a traditional oil and gas  company to decarbonize  why do you think investors seem  skeptical about the strategy  well i think the interesting thing ian  and i'm very glad that we did what we  did  uh 12 months ago is is that society in  the world has moved  towards us rather than away from us uh  over the past 12 months and that just  continues to happen each and every  day whether it's the us rejoining paris  and then  ratcheting up its ndc ambitions  just last week whether it's china coming  out with a 26 target  this is going one direction and one  direction only and in the past 12 months  because of the ambition that we laid out  the world's coming together towards our  strategy rather than further away from  it  what investors i think have questioned  ian is can we deliver  competitive cash returns to them while  at the same time  transitioning the company and that's a  very fair question and today's results  i hope really put put that point  to bed we are delivering competitive  cash returns  18 billion net reduc reduction in net  debt means that we can now start  our buyback program 500 million dollars  in the second quarter  and at the same time over the coming  years we believe we can get those  distributions back to the pre-pandemic  levels  in a moderate price environment at the  same time  we're making progress on that strategy  so the question isn't  uh is one right or one wrong it's can  you do both at the same time  and i think today's results prove that  we can  now cop 26 is coming up later this year  in glasgow are there specific  outcomes that you would hope to see look  i think there are a number of things  that  uh that need to happen clearly um  we need to all ratchet up our ambitions  and you saw that with the climate summit  happening  in the u.s uh this past week but a few  other things that  i think are important and that would be  very helpful coming out of cop  one is that we need everyone on the  pitch ian  green companies low-carbon zero-carbon  companies are fantastic  but regardless of the growth rates  they're not going to solve the world's  problems what the world needs  is greening companies and by greening i  mean companies like bp who are carbon  intensive today but  who have net zero ambitions mark carney  says if you want to solve climate  go to where the emissions are well the  emissions are here we're transitioning  how do we encourage and support greening  companies that's one  the second is clearly it's all about the  developing world  how do we help them make these changes  that are so necessary  and thirdly obviously the holy grail  here is a global price on carbon  something that's very very difficult to  do but something that we've been  advocating for decades and in fact just  last week  in washington state in the united states  a bill has passed that we are  what we were advocated strongly for  which puts a price in carbon in that  state so  they're the sorts of things ian that  would be super helpful  out of uh cops cop 26. i mean you're  clearly very sincere about this i think  most of the other european operators are  as well what about those american plays  can you ever see them going green in the  same way  well look i think we all have different  strategies and i think you know  sometimes we  we we like to talk about a u.s and an  american divide  i don't think of it like that i think  that companies rightly have different  strategies  i can't comment on other people's  strategies i can only comment  on our own strategy and i have to say  that you know  each day with time i feel better and  better about the strategy that we've  chosen  i think investors every day are  beginning to understand that strategy  better and better and you've seen that i  think in our share price performance  from the beginning of this year so i've  got faith in what we're doing and others  will make the choices that they feel is  right for them  and a brief word please on supply and  demand uh  where you see it going in the next  quarter well look i think there's a lot  of uh  uncertainty out there just because uh  the us and the uk are  sort of coming out the pandemic does not  mean that the world is coming out of the  pandemic  we've got a humanitarian uh crisis in  india that you know  a lot about we've got 7 000 employees  there we just lost two employees in the  last  week so that's clearly the focus there  brazil we've also lost many many  employees there  so i think there is uncertainty on the  demand outlook but it is very strong  in america it's very strong in china and  of course  we've got the stabilizing influence of  opec who has both the capacity  and the desire it seems to hold the  market in that sort of 60 to 70 dollar  world so  lots of uncertainty as usual the  stabilizing influence of opec in the  background  we feel quite good about the outlook for  price  all right thanks very much indeed for  sparing the time to speak to us today  it's much appreciated  thank you

